WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.276
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:07.680
BUKOLA AYODELE:
Today, I'll show you

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:09.570
how to use object
versioning with Cloud

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:13.860
Storage and some examples of
working with versioned objects.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.650
So as you can see, I've got a
storage bucket already set up

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.890
named tiny_homes, and
I've put a few photos

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:23.070
of my favorite tiny
homes in the bucket.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.190
So using our console, what we
want to do is we want to type

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:34.140
in the command gsutil versioning
set on gs://tiny_homes.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.530
This will enable versioning
for the tiny_homes bucket.

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.180
That tells Cloud
Storage to create

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:41.580
a new version of
an object each time

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.870
the live version of the object
is overwritten or deleted.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:47.940
If I want to stop
versioning, all I have to do

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:49.485
is write this command--

00:00:49.485 --> 00:00:54.960
gsutil versioning set
off gs://tiny_homes.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:58.890
If I ever need to see whether
or not versioning is enabled,

00:00:58.890 --> 00:01:00.810
it's very easy to
figure that out.

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.640
We can just quickly
write this command,

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:07.290
called gsutil versioning
get gs://tiny_homes.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.720
And as you can see,
if we get enabled,

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.040
this means that versioning
is enabled for this bucket.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.830
Now let's work with
some versioned objects.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:20.820
Here is my tiny_home's bucket
filled with tiny home pictures.

00:01:20.820 --> 00:01:23.760
I have uploaded multiple
versions of tiny home

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:25.260
to this bucket.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.960
Only the most recent version
is visible in the bucket,

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:29.730
but I can see all
the versions that

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.545
have existed with this command.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.220
If I want to access any version
that's not the most recent,

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:40.350
all I need to do is just to
append the generation number

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.410
to the object, like this.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.360
There, now you have it.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.390
You, too, can manipulate
past or present versions

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:50.070
of your storage objects.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.420
[MUSIC PLAYING]

